\title{Twenty extremal graphs of small order}
\author{Human Compression project}
\date{13 October 2026}
\begin{document}\maketitle
An $\egr(n,k,g,\lambda)$ graph is a finite connected simple graph
on $n$ vertices, regular of degree $k$, with girth $g$ and precisely
$\lambda$ girth cycles through every edge. Let $n(k,g,\lambda)$ be
the least possible order. We recover twenty entries of Tables 1--4
in the fixed manuscript of Goedgebeur and Jooken~\cite{source}.
The results and their positive examples belong to those authors and
the earlier work they cite. Here the finite classifications follow
from one complete family of regular-graph lists and one cycle count.

\begin{theorem}\label{main}
For each row below, $n(k,g,\lambda)=n$, and exactly one isomorphism
class occurs at this order.
\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{rrrr@{\qquad}rrrr}
\toprule
$k$&$g$&$\lambda$&$n$&$k$&$g$&$\lambda$&$n$\\\midrule
3&3&2&4&4&4&9&8\\
3&4&2&8&5&3&2&12\\
3&4&4&6&5&3&4&6\\
3&5&4&10&5&4&12&12\\
4&3&1&9&5&4&16&10\\
4&3&2&6&6&3&2&12\\
4&3&3&5&6&3&3&9\\
4&4&2&13&6&3&4&8\\
4&4&5&10&6&3&5&7\\
4&4&6&10&6&4&25&12\\\bottomrule
\end{tabular}\end{center}
In addition, $n(4,4,3)=14$.
\end{theorem}
The last assertion does not classify the graphs at order fourteen.
The theorem at $(4,3,1)$ was already recovered by our cycle-incidence
argument; its occurrence here is an overlapping verification, with
no additional result credit. The other nineteen classification entries
and the exclusion at order twelve are the present contribution to the
recovery. None is represented as a previously open conjecture.

\paragraph{The acquired lower bounds and positive examples.}
We use the actual bounds and divisibility arguments in the accepted
note \emph{Lower bounds from short cycles}~\cite{lower}, not the
source table as a mathematical premise. They give the stated lower
order in each of the twenty rows. The accepted positive-witness
component retains an example of exactly that order in every row,
and an $\egr(14,4,4,3)$ graph. Its graph data are from the source
authors' repository at commit
\texttt{41022f4a3ad84e4502f8889c5b64132225411ca2}.
That repository edition is later than the fixed June 2024 manuscript;
only the literal examples, already checked, are acquired here.
The present computation checks each returned graph against its
particular source example by a recorded vertex bijection.

\paragraph{Complete regular-graph lists.}
We acquire the general counting and canonical-form arguments in
\emph{A small incidence classification for unique pentagons}~\cite{pentagon}.
They apply to arbitrary finite degree sequences, not just the
degree-five bases used in that application. To specify the interface,
let $F(d_1,\ldots,d_n)$ count simple graphs on a fixed labelled vertex
set with those degrees. Deleting the first vertex gives
\[
 F(d_1,\ldots,d_n)=
 \sum_{\substack{S\subseteq\{2,\ldots,n\}\\|S|=d_1}}
 F\bigl(d_2-\mathbf1_{2\in S},\ldots,d_n-\mathbf1_{n\in S}\bigr).
\]
The empty sequence has value one; impossible degrees and odd degree
sums have value zero. Each summand records precisely one neighbour
set of the deleted labelled vertex. Sorting a residual degree sequence
merely relabels the remaining vertices and preserves its count.

The acquired canonical procedure refines an ordered partition by
neighbour counts, individualizes every vertex of a selected cell,
and compares the complete adjacency words at its leaves. The least
word is an isomorphism invariant that distinguishes nonisomorphic
graphs. Its leaf multiplicity is the exact automorphism order.
Disconnected components are included, using the acquired product
formula and the factorials for permutable equal components.
These statements and their proofs are acquired without changing
the implementation in \texttt{base\_cover5.py}.

For a proposed list of pairwise nonisomorphic $d$-regular graphs,
the equality
\[
             \sum_H\frac{n!}{|\operatorname{Aut}H|}
                  =F(\underbrace{d,\ldots,d}_{n})
\tag{1}
\]
certifies completeness: the disjoint labelling orbits on the left
are subsets of the labelled graphs counted on the right. The
checker verifies degree, simplicity, distinct canonical words and
every summand before testing (1). The candidate provider's claim
of completeness is therefore unnecessary.

Complementation gives an isomorphism-preserving bijection between
$k$-regular and $(n-1-k)$-regular graphs on $n$ vertices. It also
preserves their automorphism groups. We may consequently classify
using degree $d=\min(k,n-1-k)$ and complement each candidate when
needed. No connectivity restriction is imposed on these base lists:
the complement of a disconnected base can be a connected target.
The resulting targets are checked for connectivity separately.

