\paragraph{The cycle-counting test.}
\begin{lemma}
Let $uv$ be an edge of a simple graph $X$. Let $d$ be the distance
between $u$ and $v$ in $X-uv$, or infinity if there is no such path.
The shortest cycles of $X$ containing $uv$ have length $d+1$, and
their number is the number of shortest $u$--$v$ paths in $X-uv$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Deleting $uv$ from a cycle through that edge gives a simple path
between its endpoints. Adding the edge reverses the operation.
A shortest path is simple, since deleting a repeated-vertex segment
would shorten it. Thus the two operations give inverse bijections
between the shortest objects. Fixing the orientation from $u$ to $v$
counts each unoriented cycle exactly once.
\end{proof}

Breadth-first search computes these distances and path numbers
with exact integers. Initially the starting vertex has distance zero
and count one. When an edge reaches a previously unseen vertex, its
distance is set to one more than the current distance. Every edge
from distance $j$ to distance $j+1$ adds the count at its first
endpoint to the count at its second. Induction on $j$ proves the
invariant: the count at a vertex is the number of shortest paths
from the starting vertex. All vertices at distance $j$ are processed
before those at distance $j+1$, so no contribution arrives late.

Apply the lemma to every edge, and take the minimum returned cycle
length $g$. An edge whose own shortest cycle is longer than $g$
lies in zero girth cycles. Otherwise its returned path count is
exactly its number of girth cycles. Hence a connected regular
candidate is accepted for $(g,\lambda)$ precisely when the minimum
is $g$ and all these counts equal the positive integer $\lambda$.
A bridge receives count zero. An acyclic graph is rejected.
These tests make no transitivity assumption.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
The certified lists and complementation include every regular graph
at each stated order, exactly once up to isomorphism for each degree.
The cycle test is invariant under isomorphism and is exact by the
lemma. It returns precisely one graph for each of the twenty rows.
The retained source bijections verify the positive identifications
directly. The acquired lower bounds therefore prove the twenty
minima and their asserted uniqueness.

For $(k,g,\lambda)=(4,4,3)$, the acquired tree bound gives $n\ge11$.
Counting incidences of edges with quadrangles gives $8\mid12n$,
so $n$ is even and $n\ge12$. The complete degree-four list on
twelve vertices has no graph passing the $(4,3)$ cycle test.
Thus $n\ge14$. The acquired positive example of order fourteen
proves equality.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Certificates and attribution.}
The complete regular lists, every canonical word and automorphism
order, all fifteen labelled counts, the accepted graphs and their
twenty source bijections are retained. Every bijection is checked
against every pair of vertices; the map provider is not trusted.
An independently organized traversal lists simple cycles from their
least vertex and retains one of the two orientations. On all
$33\,867$ labelled graphs of orders one through six it agrees with
the breadth-first test, including disconnected graphs and bridges.
The same traversal checks each returned positive graph. The
acquired canonical and degree-recurrence implementation retains
its previous exhaustive small controls and distinct review.

The source results retain their original attribution. The common
coverage certificate is reused explicitly; the present work supplies
its application to these twenty classifications, the additional
lower-order exclusion, exact finite calculations and source maps.
The source has many remaining numerical obligations. This component
does not supply a whole-original recovery, a new open-conjecture
answer, a manuscript compression factor or a Lean formalization.

